  "topic": "Virtual Function Table (vtable) Mechanism and Dynamic Dispatch",
  "explanation": "In C++, when a class contains virtual functions, the compiler implements dynamic dispatch through a vtable—a hidden table of function pointers associated with each polymorphic object. This vtable enables runtime resolution of function calls, allowing the program to invoke the correct overridden function based on the actual object's type, not the static type. Internally, each polymorphic object contains a pointer to its class's vtable, and function calls are dispatched by dereferencing this pointer and jumping to the appropriate function address. This mechanism is fundamental to supporting runtime polymorphism in the C++ object model, providing flexibility at the cost of additional memory overhead and indirection during function calls.",
  "syntax": "For a class with virtual functions:\n\nclass ClassName {\npublic:\n    virtual ReturnType FunctionName(Parameters);\n    // ...\n};\n\n// Compiler-generated internal structures:\n// - Each class with virtual functions has a vtable: a static array of function pointers.\n// - Each object of such class contains an implicit pointer to its vtable (commonly called vptr).\n// - Function calls to virtual functions are implemented as:\n//   *(object->vptr[index]) (object, other args);",
